## 5.3. Certificate database structure

Every certificate must have a structured record.

Required fields:

- Unique certificate ID.
- Official name.
- Short name.
- Category.
- Subcategory.
- Language or subject.
- Issuing organization.
- Official website.
- Examination format.
- Available levels.
- Minimum score.
- Maximum score.
- Passing requirements, if applicable.
- Score interpretation.
- Validity period.
- Approximate examination fee.
- Currency.
- Country or region availability.
- Examination frequency.
- Registration information.
- Official preparation resources.
- Last verified date.
- Information source.
- Information reliability status.

Important:

Not every examination has the same scoring system.

Examples:

IELTS: 0–9 band scale.

JLPT: N5–N1 levels, with examination-specific scoring and passing requirements.

SAT: 400–1600 total score.

TOPIK: Different score structures for TOPIK I and II.

AWS and professional certifications: Different examination-specific passing and validity rules.

Never force every certificate into one generic scoring model.

Support:

- Numeric scores.
- Levels.
- Pass/Fail.
- Grades.
- Awards.
- Multiple score components.

## 5.4. Validity and examination fees

